{
  "dom_id": "step-q47138",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "A group of randomly selected students from School A was asked about whether or not they use TV or the internet to access the local news. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nThere are $100$ students in School B. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both schools, \nestimate the number of School B students that access the local news either from TV or the internet, but not both.",
  "worked_solution": "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student gets the local news either from TV or the internet but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"No internet\"** or the column **\"TV\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(150),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{30+15}{150}=\\frac{45}{150}=0.3.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student gets the local news from TV or the internet but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of School B that get their local news either from TV or the internet but not both, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(100)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100\\cdot p \\\\  & =100\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
